### 5. Consider passing the player’s guess

The current `/streetview/ai-review` request only sends the image ID. That allows the AI to describe visible clues, but it does not know where the player guessed.

For more personalized feedback, also send:

```js
body: JSON.stringify({
  imageId: window.imageId,
  mode: "review",
  guessLat: lastGuess.lat,
  guessLng: lastGuess.lng,
});
```

You would then update `streetviewRouter.js` to include the guess in the prompt, for example:

> The player guessed latitude X and longitude Y. Explain which visible clues could have helped them make a more accurate guess. Do not reveal hidden metadata or use GPS data from the image.

The existing results response already contains the actual location, but avoid trusting coordinates supplied directly by the browser. If you add personalized feedback, validate the round and retrieve the player’s stored guess server-side where possible.

One important distinction: your current `resultsSection` is the end-of-round screen, while `gameOverSection` is only shown after the final round. Put the AI review in `resultsSection` if users should be able to review every round.

> I'm a bit confused. What does showReviewStreetview and restoreGameStreetview do? Do they actually move the streetview element? So by appending the child, it removes it from the place it used to be?
>
> And also what does the requestAnimationFrame do ?

Yes. `appendChild()` moves the existing DOM element.

For example:

```js
reviewSlot.appendChild(streetviewContainer);
```

If `streetviewContainer` currently lives inside `#mapContainer`, the browser automatically removes it from `#mapContainer` and places it inside `#review-streetview-slot`.

So:

```js
function showReviewStreetview() {
  reviewSlot.appendChild(streetviewContainer);
}
```

moves Street View into the review screen.

Then:

```js
function restoreGameStreetview() {
  mapContainer.appendChild(streetviewContainer);
}
```

moves the same Street View element back to the normal game screen.

It does not create a copy. This is useful because the existing Mapillary viewer, image, controls, and event handlers remain associated with the same element. Cloning it would generally not preserve the viewer correctly.

`requestAnimationFrame()` tells the browser:

> Run this callback just before the next screen repaint.

After moving the Street View element, its size and position may not have been recalculated immediately. Therefore:

```js
requestAnimationFrame(() => {
  streetviewViewer.resize();
});
```

waits until the browser has processed the DOM move and layout changes, then asks the viewer to recalculate its dimensions.

Without it, the viewer might still think it has the old width and height, causing problems such as:

- a blank Street View area;
- incorrect sizing;
- controls appearing in the wrong position;
- the viewer only appearing after manually resizing the window.

In short:

- `appendChild()` moves the element.
- `showReviewStreetview()` moves it to the review layout.
- `restoreGameStreetview()` moves it back.
- `requestAnimationFrame()` gives the browser time to update layout before resizing the viewer.

> In the catch block, does every error object have a .message attribute?

No. JavaScript allows anything to be thrown:

```js
throw "Something went wrong";
throw { code: 500 };
throw null;
```

Only actual `Error` objects reliably have a `.message` property.

Use a safe fallback:

```js
catch (error) {
  const message =
    error instanceof Error
      ? error.message
      : String(error || "An unknown error occurred");

  aiReviewStatus.textContent = message;
}
```

In your own code, prefer throwing `Error` objects:

```js
throw new Error("Unable to generate AI review.");
```

Then `error.message` is safe in normal circumstances. Note that `fetch()` failures usually produce an `Error`, while API errors from `response.json()` are just ordinary objects unless you explicitly convert them to `Error`.

