> for an error object, does it always have a .error or a .message property? is it simply because of logic done earlier that results in it having these propreties?

No. JavaScript does not guarantee that a thrown value has either `.error` or `.message`.

Anything can be thrown:

```js
throw "Something failed";
throw { error: "API failed" };
throw { message: "Validation failed" };
throw null;
```

The properties exist only because the value being thrown contains them, or because it is an `Error` object:

```js
throw new Error("Something failed");
// error.message === "Something failed"
```

In your code, this:

```js
throw new Error(aiReview.error || "AI review failed");
```

creates an `Error` object, so `.message` exists. The original API response may have had `.error`, but after wrapping it with `new Error(...)`, the useful property is `.message`.

A safe catch block is:

```js
catch (error) {
  const message =
    error instanceof Error
      ? error.message
      : String(error || "Unknown error");

  aiReviewStatus.textContent = message;
}
```

So yes: whether `.error` or `.message` exists depends on the earlier logic and on exactly what was thrown.
